Assuming we have an integer `n`, here's a simple solution using bitwise operators (without loops or recursion) to check whether it is a power of three:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 9; // change this value for any other test case
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(n));
    }

    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n < 1 || n % 3!= 0)
            return false;
        return n == Math.pow(3, countBits(n) - 1);
    }

    public static int countBits(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        while ((n & 1) == 1) {
            count++;
            n >>= 1;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

Here's how the code works:
* We first check if the number is less than 1 or not divisible by 3. If so, it can't be a power of 3 (since powers of 3 must always be positive). Otherwise, we calculate the power of 3 required and then shift right (dividing by 3 repeatedly) until it becomes zero. The difference between the original number and the shifted result gives us the bits in the number that were used to power 3.

Please note that this implementation has some edge cases that could potentially lead to errors, but assuming integers don't go over 32-bit range, this should work for all positive non-zero 32-bit signed integers (`-2^(31)` to `(2^30)-1`). For larger numbers, this method would need additional bit-shifting operations, as there might not be enough bits in a single 32-bit variable.

